    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:C6QQ1ofri1GI9wNPE631Jv ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "In 3 AH, Quraysh returned with 3,000 men. The Prophet ﷺ placed fifty archers on a hill with orders never to leave. When the Muslims began winning, most archers left for the spoils. Khalid ibn al-Walid circled behind, and the Muslims were thrown into confusion. A rumour spread that the Prophet ﷺ had been killed. He was wounded in the face and his tooth broken, while a small band of Companions shielded him."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:6AskWNH0iaEYmAB9YQ72NR
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label """Anas ibn al-Nadr: "I Smell Paradise\""""@en ;
    skos:notation 22 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:u6EuRgezVSaYn21j4FQVY2 ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:u6EuRgezVSaYn21j4FQVY2
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label """Anas ibn al-Nadr: "I Smell Paradise\""""@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 22" ;
    skos:notation 22 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:6AskWNH0iaEYmAB9YQ72NR ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Anas ibn al-Nadr missed Badr and vowed that if Allah let him witness another battle, Allah would see what he would do. At Uhud, when others wavered, he said to Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, "Paradise! By the Lord of the Ka'bah, I smell its scent beyond Uhud." He was found with more than eighty wounds, recognised only by his sister from his fingertips. The verse "Among the believers are men who were true to their covenant with Allah" (33:23) is linked to him."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:yMh0mkq1zRLFDo5Zn0k4Bf
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Trench (al-Khandaq)"@en ;
    skos:notation 23 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:0FZcdLk4ilzVXWOssiOYat ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:0FZcdLk4ilzVXWOssiOYat
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of the Trench (al-Khandaq)"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 23" ;
    skos:notation 23 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:yMh0mkq1zRLFDo5Zn0k4Bf ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "In 5 AH, about 10,000 confederates marched on Madinah. On Salman's advice, the Muslims dug a trench across the open northern side in freezing cold and hunger. When a rock defeated them, the Prophet ﷺ struck it three times, and with each spark foretold the conquest of Syria, Persia and Yemen. After nearly a month, discord among the enemy and a fierce wind broke the siege."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:RI73i48wVHlLPKxUrt4O3G
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh"@en ;
    skos:notation 24 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:YPNqSbpgfPdmmxrIqxG6Sb ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:YPNqSbpgfPdmmxrIqxG6Sb
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 24" ;
    skos:notation 24 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:RI73i48wVHlLPKxUrt4O3G ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Wounded at the Trench, Sa'd prayed that he would not die until his heart was at peace regarding Banu Qurayzah, who had broken their treaty in the Muslims' most dangerous hour. Their case was referred to his judgment, which the Prophet ﷺ said matched the judgment of Allah. His wound then reopened and he died. The Prophet ﷺ said, "The Throne of the Most Merciful shook at the death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh," and seventy thousand angels attended his funeral."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:lW0F3Leiip7MIlnVloWdy9
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Hudaybiyah"@en ;
    skos:notation 25 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:gKsN3nOnH7D83ITpix6jbb ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:gKsN3nOnH7D83ITpix6jbb
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Treaty of Hudaybiyah"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 25" ;
    skos:notation 25 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:lW0F3Leiip7MIlnVloWdy9 ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """In 6 AH, the Prophet ﷺ set out with about 1,400 Companions for Umrah but was stopped at Hudaybiyah. He sent Uthman to negotiate, and when a rumour said Uthman was killed, the Companions pledged under a tree to stand firm. Quraysh then sent Suhayl ibn Amr, and a truce was signed with terms that seemed harsh to the Muslims. Allah called it "a clear victory" in Surah al-Fath, and within two years Islam spread more than ever before."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:8AsdDzdoVrdOUx44sOKhF5
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Conquest of Khaybar"@en ;
    skos:notation 26 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:KjaprXGlhUmGqKmHbyydX0 ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:KjaprXGlhUmGqKmHbyydX0
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Conquest of Khaybar"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 26" ;
    skos:notation 26 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:8AsdDzdoVrdOUx44sOKhF5 ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """In 7 AH, the Muslims besieged the fortresses of Khaybar. After days of failed attempts, the Prophet ﷺ said, "Tomorrow I will give the flag to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and whom Allah and His Messenger love." Everyone hoped for it, but he called Ali, who had sore eyes. The Prophet ﷺ applied his saliva, the eyes were healed, and the fortress fell."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:EgVEgSWlpu8iKlVoPSSRor
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mu'tah"@en ;
    skos:notation 27 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:I8wUopw87xOVx52FSd4uMD ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:I8wUopw87xOVx52FSd4uMD
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Mu'tah"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 27" ;
    skos:notation 27 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:EgVEgSWlpu8iKlVoPSSRor ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """In 8 AH, 3,000 Muslims met a far larger Byzantine force. The Prophet ﷺ had named three commanders in turn: Zayd, then Ja'far, then Abdullah ibn Rawahah. All three were martyred. Khalid ibn al-Walid took the flag and withdrew the army skilfully. In Madinah, with tears in his eyes, the Prophet ﷺ announced each death before the news arrived, then said, "A sword from the swords of Allah took the flag.\""""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:dFiLtnVBpwiuM0lNJs8Wur
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Conquest of Makkah"@en ;
    skos:notation 28 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:DKQslmTDQOMenHY9HeiQw0 ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:DKQslmTDQOMenHY9HeiQw0
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Conquest of Makkah"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 28" ;
    skos:notation 28 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:dFiLtnVBpwiuM0lNJs8Wur ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """When Quraysh's allies broke the Hudaybiyah truce, the Prophet ﷺ marched with 10,000 men in Ramadan of 8 AH. Makkah fell almost without fighting. He entered with his head bowed in humility, cleared 360 idols from around the Ka'bah reciting "Truth has come and falsehood has vanished" (17:81), and told his former persecutors, "No blame on you today. Go, you are free.\""""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:5cRVmhPCcc7zxotTXpIGQs
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hunayn"@en ;
    skos:notation 29 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:BBWyQqyHSEWp5u7l5oloHR ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:BBWyQqyHSEWp5u7l5oloHR
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Battle of Hunayn"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 29" ;
    skos:notation 29 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:5cRVmhPCcc7zxotTXpIGQs ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Weeks after the conquest, 12,000 Muslims met the Hawazin. Some were pleased by their numbers (9:25). Ambushed in a narrow valley, many fled, but the Prophet ﷺ rode forward saying, "I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of Abd al-Muttalib." Al-Abbas called the Companions back, and they won. When the spoils went to new Muslims of Quraysh, some Ansar felt hurt, and the Prophet ﷺ told them, "Are you not pleased that people go home with sheep and camels, and you go home with the Messenger of Allah?" They wept until their beards were wet."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:qOVrmxwiGDJjo74MEVF5H4
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Expedition of Tabuk"@en ;
    skos:notation 30 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:039sxVjoPIpklTujCCARAU ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:039sxVjoPIpklTujCCARAU
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Expedition of Tabuk"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 30" ;
    skos:notation 30 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:qOVrmxwiGDJjo74MEVF5H4 ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """In 9 AH, in severe heat and poverty, the Prophet ﷺ called for an expedition to Tabuk against Byzantium, known as the "army of hardship." Companions gave all they could. Uthman equipped a large part of the army, and the Prophet ﷺ said, "Nothing Uthman does after today will harm him." The army waited twenty days at Tabuk, no battle took place, and the tribes of the north made treaties."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:H0mClpFAfGco9zqw29MOgt
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Ka'b ibn Malik and the Three Who Stayed Behind"@en ;
    skos:notation 31 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:CZ0F6BVk3A7rKZzQAXaq6V ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:CZ0F6BVk3A7rKZzQAXaq6V
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Ka'b ibn Malik and the Three Who Stayed Behind"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 3, story 31" ;
    skos:notation 31 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:0F5OSXadhRL2SJDckLGkOu ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:H0mClpFAfGco9zqw29MOgt ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Ka'b had no excuse for missing Tabuk and refused to invent one. The Prophet ﷺ ordered the Muslims not to speak to him or his two companions for fifty days. The king of Ghassan wrote inviting him over; Ka'b threw the letter into an oven. On the fiftieth morning, Allah revealed their forgiveness (9:118), and a man ran up the mountain shouting the good news."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:PXDP7KOTRK8uh27eG6fCNT
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Uthman and the Well of Rumah"@en ;
    skos:notation 32 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:YZguEgwnJYdasXkmTyk0ps ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:YZguEgwnJYdasXkmTyk0ps
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Uthman and the Well of Rumah"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 32" ;
    skos:notation 32 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:PXDP7KOTRK8uh27eG6fCNT ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """When the Muslims reached Madinah, sweet water was scarce, and the well of Rumah belonged to a man who sold its water at a high price. The Prophet ﷺ said, "Whoever buys the well of Rumah and makes it free for the Muslims will have a better one in Paradise." Uthman bought it, first half then all, and made it a free endowment for everyone."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:xujWoW6WQU8LGm20uG1p0r
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Slander Against Aishah (al-Ifk)"@en ;
    skos:notation 33 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:KkDIQaxEdLkuBzpi99BuRz ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:KkDIQaxEdLkuBzpi99BuRz
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Slander Against Aishah (al-Ifk)"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 33" ;
    skos:notation 33 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:xujWoW6WQU8LGm20uG1p0r ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Returning from an expedition, Aishah left the camp to search for a lost necklace, and the army departed without her. Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal found her and brought her back, leading the camel on foot. Hypocrites spread a lie about her, and for a month Madinah was troubled. Then Allah revealed ten verses of Surah al-Nur declaring her innocence."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:jAM6Iv8nQs3TNo71bhWhwx
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Zayd ibn Harithah Chooses the Prophet ﷺ"@en ;
    skos:notation 34 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:kPYIwTu9171zoWLZ5hUN4O ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:kPYIwTu9171zoWLZ5hUN4O
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Zayd ibn Harithah Chooses the Prophet ﷺ"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 34" ;
    skos:notation 34 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:jAM6Iv8nQs3TNo71bhWhwx ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Zayd was kidnapped as a child and sold in Makkah; Khadijah gave him to the Prophet ﷺ before prophethood. Years later his father and uncle found him and came to buy his freedom. The Prophet ﷺ let Zayd choose freely. Zayd chose to stay, saying he would choose no one over him. The Prophet ﷺ then declared him his son before Quraysh."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:d4rSHjOPFqyy1YnVivxf3R
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Abu Hurayrah and the Cup of Milk"@en ;
    skos:notation 35 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:sD5ZgZPhf0oJAdRS9YeNBL ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:sD5ZgZPhf0oJAdRS9YeNBL
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Abu Hurayrah and the Cup of Milk"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 35" ;
    skos:notation 35 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:d4rSHjOPFqyy1YnVivxf3R ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Abu Hurayrah lived in the mosque with the poor of al-Suffah. One day, faint with hunger, he sat on the road hoping someone would invite him. The Prophet ﷺ saw his face, brought him home and sent him to call all the people of al-Suffah to share a single cup of milk. Abu Hurayrah served everyone first, then the Prophet ﷺ told him, "Drink," again and again, until he said, "I can drink no more." Abu Hurayrah also wept that his mother refused Islam. The Prophet ﷺ prayed for her, and he returned home to find her accepting Islam."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:wFqWq7VtMmMmZ5LGIoryKM
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Abu Talhah and Umm Sulaym: Generosity and Patience"@en ;
    skos:notation 36 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:VYkPQVkTN4cvXzwsHUdY6u ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:VYkPQVkTN4cvXzwsHUdY6u
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Abu Talhah and Umm Sulaym: Generosity and Patience"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 36" ;
    skos:notation 36 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:wFqWq7VtMmMmZ5LGIoryKM ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "A hungry guest came to the Prophet ﷺ, and Abu Talhah took him home with only enough food for their children. Umm Sulaym put the children to sleep and dimmed the lamp so the guest would not see they were not eating. In the morning, the Prophet ﷺ said Allah had admired their deed, and verse 59:9 was revealed. When their young son later died, Umm Sulaym prepared her husband's meal before gently telling him, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed for blessing on their night. They were given a son, Abdullah, whose children all memorised the Qur'an."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:2FL3PHoeGZ2gycJaBXxxPf
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Sawad ibn Ghaziyyah at Badr"@en ;
    skos:notation 37 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:twsGe4sUSY4Ct0n6VytMrB ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:twsGe4sUSY4Ct0n6VytMrB
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Sawad ibn Ghaziyyah at Badr"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 37" ;
    skos:notation 37 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:2FL3PHoeGZ2gycJaBXxxPf ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """As the Prophet ﷺ straightened the ranks at Badr with an arrow, he poked Sawad in the stomach. Sawad said, "You have hurt me; let me take retribution." The Prophet ﷺ uncovered his stomach. Sawad embraced him and kissed it, saying, "I wanted my last contact in this world to be my skin touching yours." The Prophet ﷺ prayed for him."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:7KikJdnZz6FDegJOBhbf4Q
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Julaybib"@en ;
    skos:notation 38 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:qSPW4bJtg1WUZb7BAze6gA ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:qSPW4bJtg1WUZb7BAze6gA
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Julaybib"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 38" ;
    skos:notation 38 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:7KikJdnZz6FDegJOBhbf4Q ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Julaybib was a poor man of humble appearance with no family. The Prophet ﷺ himself asked an Ansari family for their daughter's hand on his behalf. The parents hesitated, but the daughter said, "Will you reject the Messenger's request? Send me to him." Soon after, Julaybib was martyred in battle having killed seven. The Prophet ﷺ searched for him, laid him on his own arms, and said, "He is from me and I am from him.\""""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:EYQmZnQKs5HbkB4krDKuFI
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Mu'adh ibn Jabal Sent to Yemen"@en ;
    skos:notation 39 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:OUM7jCASiexLK9f4uzKsFp ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:OUM7jCASiexLK9f4uzKsFp
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Mu'adh ibn Jabal Sent to Yemen"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 39" ;
    skos:notation 39 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:EYQmZnQKs5HbkB4krDKuFI ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """When sending Mu'adh to Yemen, the Prophet ﷺ asked how he would judge. Mu'adh said by the Book of Allah, then the Sunnah, then by his own reasoning. The Prophet ﷺ praised Allah. Another time he took Mu'adh's hand and said, "O Mu'adh, by Allah, I love you," and taught him to say after every prayer, "O Allah, help me to remember You, thank You and worship You well." Walking beside Mu'adh's mount as he left, he told him he might not see him again. Mu'adh wept."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:aNFT92sap1T6hghNnkdiPR
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum and Surah Abasa"@en ;
    skos:notation 40 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:siLNDxtGcoaatyyVi2XbQU ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:siLNDxtGcoaatyyVi2XbQU
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum and Surah Abasa"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 40" ;
    skos:notation 40 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:aNFT92sap1T6hghNnkdiPR ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """The Prophet ﷺ was inviting Quraysh leaders when the blind Companion Ibn Umm Maktum came asking to be taught. The Prophet ﷺ frowned and turned away, hoping to win the leaders. Allah revealed "He frowned and turned away" (80:1). Afterwards the Prophet ﷺ honoured him, saying, "Welcome to the one about whom my Lord reproached me," and made him deputy in Madinah many times."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:Im9a3UjJrKuCqf90ul49lv
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Usamah's Intercession and Justice for All"@en ;
    skos:notation 41 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:yMahI8vk80IlalOF7ndpOu ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:yMahI8vk80IlalOF7ndpOu
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Usamah's Intercession and Justice for All"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 41" ;
    skos:notation 41 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:Im9a3UjJrKuCqf90ul49lv ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """A woman of the noble Makhzum clan committed theft. Quraysh, worried for their status, asked Usamah, the Prophet's beloved, to intercede. The Prophet ﷺ said, "Do you intercede regarding a limit set by Allah?" He stood and declared that earlier nations were destroyed because they spared the noble and punished the weak, adding, "If Fatimah the daughter of Muhammad stole, I would cut her hand.\""""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:onjWBs7kSZl2078NhJhf9b
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Abu Dharr Accepts Islam"@en ;
    skos:notation 42 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:ARvCdPZGhXBXmKXzTnCM7u ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:ARvCdPZGhXBXmKXzTnCM7u
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Abu Dharr Accepts Islam"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 42" ;
    skos:notation 42 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:onjWBs7kSZl2078NhJhf9b ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "Hearing of a prophet in Makkah, Abu Dharr of Ghifar sent his brother, then went himself. He sat in the mosque for days, afraid to ask. Ali noticed the stranger and quietly led him to the Prophet ﷺ, where he accepted Islam. Abu Dharr then shouted the testimony of faith at the Ka'bah and was beaten until al-Abbas warned Quraysh that Ghifar lay on their trade route. He returned home, and half his tribe accepted Islam."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:dyH3SpLyPnAi5IjEqSihEz
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Al-Tufayl ibn Amr al-Dawsi"@en ;
    skos:notation 43 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:hAyGdUGujNJmEg5vgF999l ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:hAyGdUGujNJmEg5vgF999l
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Al-Tufayl ibn Amr al-Dawsi"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 43" ;
    skos:notation 43 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:dyH3SpLyPnAi5IjEqSihEz ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Al-Tufayl, a poet and chief of Daws, was warned by Quraysh not to listen to the Prophet ﷺ, so he stuffed cotton in his ears. Then he thought, "I am an intelligent man; I can tell good from bad." He listened, accepted Islam, and asked for a sign. A light shone on his whip as he returned. When his tribe was slow to respond, the Prophet ﷺ prayed, "O Allah, guide Daws," and later eighty families came to Madinah."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:FNm2wokWxwOaQhliQAxNUE
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl's Return"@en ;
    skos:notation 44 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:Uk0z8j6FRWhSveQlZcJhh6 ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:Uk0z8j6FRWhSveQlZcJhh6
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl's Return"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 44" ;
    skos:notation 44 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:FNm2wokWxwOaQhliQAxNUE ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content "After the conquest of Makkah, Ikrimah, son of the Prophet's fiercest enemy, fled to Yemen. His wife Umm Hakim accepted Islam and secured a pardon for him. When he arrived, the Prophet ﷺ stood and greeted him warmly, and told his Companions not to insult Ikrimah's father in front of him, for insulting the dead hurts the living. Ikrimah became a devoted Muslim and was martyred at Yarmuk."@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:q46t1OKYqrdOybkABtbMAP
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Wahshi, the Killer of Hamzah"@en ;
    skos:notation 45 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:RGxCIBuJmthFMwDnG7NL6o ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:RGxCIBuJmthFMwDnG7NL6o
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Wahshi, the Killer of Hamzah"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 45" ;
    skos:notation 45 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:q46t1OKYqrdOybkABtbMAP ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Wahshi, an Abyssinian slave, killed Hamzah at Uhud in return for his freedom. After the conquest of Makkah he feared for his life, then learned the Prophet ﷺ did not kill those who came to Islam. He came and accepted Islam. The Prophet ﷺ forgave him but asked, "Can you keep your face away from me?" Years later, Wahshi killed the false prophet Musaylimah at Yamamah with the same spear."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:rWgR8NIPxBgyHsCQ48MqXN
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Thumamah ibn Uthal"@en ;
    skos:notation 46 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:E8KrsTuZRqmRo5hhioZBeZ ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:E8KrsTuZRqmRo5hhioZBeZ
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Thumamah ibn Uthal"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 46" ;
    skos:notation 46 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:rWgR8NIPxBgyHsCQ48MqXN ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Thumamah, a chief of Yamamah, was captured by a Muslim cavalry unit and tied to a pillar of the mosque. For three days the Prophet ﷺ asked him how he was and treated him kindly, then freed him without ransom. Thumamah bathed, returned and accepted Islam, saying, "No face on earth was more hateful to me than yours; now it is the most beloved." He then cut off grain from Yamamah to Makkah until Quraysh begged the Prophet ﷺ to intervene."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:Zmxa51brDKlSk9InW61RJ1
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Delegation of Najran and the Mubahalah"@en ;
    skos:notation 47 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:TtP57vXNFLdoWhL9ig5Jo1 ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:TtP57vXNFLdoWhL9ig5Jo1
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Delegation of Najran and the Mubahalah"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 47" ;
    skos:notation 47 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:Zmxa51brDKlSk9InW61RJ1 ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """A Christian delegation from Najran came to discuss Isa (Jesus). When they would not accept the Qur'an's teaching, Allah commanded the Prophet ﷺ to invite them to a mutual invocation of Allah's curse on the liars (3:61). He came out with Ali, Fatimah, Hasan and Husayn. The delegation declined and made a peace treaty instead. They asked for a trustworthy man, and he sent Abu Ubaydah, "the trustworthy one of this Ummah.\""""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:HRgd382CF584NW4uotthGW
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "Ali and Fatimah: Marriage and the Gift of Tasbih"@en ;
    skos:notation 48 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:LzHPOXOZ1SwiwUB6epjv7j ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:LzHPOXOZ1SwiwUB6epjv7j
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Ali and Fatimah: Marriage and the Gift of Tasbih"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 48" ;
    skos:notation 48 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:HRgd382CF584NW4uotthGW ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """Ali married Fatimah with a simple mahr from the sale of his armour. Their home was humble. Fatimah's hands were blistered from the grinding stone, and Ali's chest ached from drawing water, so they asked the Prophet ﷺ for a servant from the captives. He came to them at night and said, "Shall I tell you something better than a servant? When you go to bed, say SubhanAllah 33 times, Alhamdulillah 33 times and Allahu Akbar 34 times." Ali said he never left it afterwards."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:Xc5MZGkJOE35hT6xordrrc
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Farewell Pilgrimage"@en ;
    skos:notation 49 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:WqpdcVadx4gbM1MQ8Uugyl ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:WqpdcVadx4gbM1MQ8Uugyl
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Farewell Pilgrimage"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 49" ;
    skos:notation 49 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:Xc5MZGkJOE35hT6xordrrc ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """In 10 AH, the Prophet ﷺ made his only Hajj with over 100,000 people. At Arafah he gave his great sermon: lives, wealth and honour are sacred; usury and blood feuds of ignorance are abolished; treat women well; hold to the Book of Allah. He asked, "Have I conveyed?" and they said, "Yes," and he raised his finger to the sky: "O Allah, bear witness." Then the verse was revealed: "This day I have perfected your religion for you" (5:3)."""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ev:CDxwUJ6cALsaOk1onbaiV2
    a mrs:Event ;
    rdfs:label "The Final Illness and Passing of the Prophet ﷺ"@en ;
    skos:notation 50 ;
    crm:P67i_is_referred_to_by psg:YGp6vvjuKSZhj7NiC9B28s ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

psg:YGp6vvjuKSZhj7NiC9B28s
    a mrs:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "The Final Illness and Passing of the Prophet ﷺ"@en ;
    mrs:canonicalCitation "50 Stories (rev 7), Part 4, story 50" ;
    skos:notation 50 ;
    mrs:inWitness wit:SfERCK2TeJUtCZL9VuJnPH ;
    dct:isPartOf psg:sAwMI97kgJO0YVdW0up9qo ;
    crm:P67_refers_to ev:CDxwUJ6cALsaOk1onbaiV2 ;
    crm:P190_has_symbolic_content """In Rabi' al-Awwal of 11 AH, the Prophet ﷺ fell ill and asked to be nursed in Aishah's house. When he could no longer lead the prayer, he said, "Tell Abu Bakr to lead the people." On his last morning he lifted the curtain and smiled at the rows behind Abu Bakr. He died with his head on Aishah's chest, saying, "With the highest companion." When Umar refused to believe it, Abu Bakr recited, "Muhammad is but a messenger; messengers have passed away before him" (3:144), and said, "Whoever worshipped Muhammad, Muhammad has died; whoever worshipped Allah, Allah is alive and never dies.\""""@en ;
    mrs:coverageState mrs:cs_stub ;
    mrs:createdBy agt:99lfWZ9Al5KAc3YUZNakNh ;
    mrs:recordedAt "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

ast:4fogpK75CybPzeHDgsw0vc
